    "dates": [
      {
        "date": "2026-01-24",
        "description": "If no shortening of the characteristic period is observed within the defined time window ($\\pm 48\\text{ hours}$ centered on March 16, 2026), and the rotation remains near or above approximately 7.1 hours with no associated stabilization of jet morphology, the proposed harmonic framework is disfavored. In that case, the observed period transition from 7.75 hours to 7.14 hours must be attributed to processes unrelated to the mechanisms proposed in this work.",
        "type": {
          "id": "other",
          "title": {
            "de": "Sonstiges",
            "en": "Other"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "description": "<p><strong>Abstract:</strong> This paper presents a pre-registered harmonic prediction for the interstellar object 3I/ATLAS associated with its close approach to Jupiter on March 16, 2026. Prior to perihelion, observations indicated a characteristic rotational or wobble period of approximately 7.75 hours, which transitioned to 7.14 hours post-perihelion alongside a persistent three-jet morphology separated by roughly 120 degrees. Using an empirical ratio (<span class=\"math-inline\">$R \\approx 1.085$</span>) derived from this shift, a subsequent stable harmonic state near 6.58 hours is predicted to emerge abruptly. Alternatively, a secondary geometry-locked resonance near 5.9&ndash;6.0 hours may manifest due to structural coupling with the triple-jet configuration. The timing of this transition is predicted to coincide strictly with the object's traversal of Jupiter's Hill sphere boundary (<span class=\"math-inline\">$\\approx 53.4\\text{ million kilometers}$</span>) rather than evolving gradually with heliocentric distance, providing explicit, time-bounded criteria for confirmation or falsification.</p>",
